    "category": "Respiratory Physiology: Measurement of Residual Volume",
    "questionText": "A pulmonary laboratory determines functional residual capacity using a spirometer of known volume filled with air containing helium at a known concentration. The patient exhales normally, then immediately begins to rebreathe from the closed circuit until the helium concentration in the spirometer stops falling and reaches a stable plateau. The final helium concentration is then used to calculate the volume of gas that was in the lungs at the start of rebreathing. The reliability of the entire calculation rests on one property of helium.",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Helium is more soluble in plasma than nitrogen, so it equilibrates", "explanation": "Incorrect. Solubility in blood would be fatal to this technique: any tracer that dissolved into the pulmonary capillary blood would continue to disappear from the circuit, so the concentration would never plateau and the calculated volume would be falsely high. The method depends on the opposite property. This would be the correct answer if the test were designed to measure diffusing capacity across the alveolar membrane rather than a gas volume." },
      { "text": "Helium is inspired at 100% concentration and washes nitrogen out", "explanation": "Incorrect. This describes the nitrogen washout technique, in which 100% oxygen is inspired through a two-way valve and the nitrogen fraction of every expired breath is measured until it falls below 1.5% for three consecutive breaths. In helium dilution the tracer is present at a low known concentration and is diluted, not used to wash anything out. This would be the correct answer if the laboratory were performing nitrogen washout instead." },
      { "text": "Helium reacts with hemoglobin, allowing its uptake to be tracked", "explanation": "Incorrect. Helium is an inert noble gas and does not bind hemoglobin or participate in any chemical reaction in the body. A tracer that bound hemoglobin would be removed from the gas phase continuously and could not give a stable plateau concentration. This would be the correct answer if the tracer in question were carbon monoxide in a diffusion study." },
      { "text": "Helium compresses under thoracic pressure, obeying Boyle's law", "explanation": "Incorrect. Boyle's law (P1V1 = P2V2 at constant temperature) is the principle behind whole-body plethysmography, in which the patient pants against a shuttered mouthpiece inside a sealed box and thoracic gas volume is derived from pressure changes. Helium dilution is a concentration method, not a pressure-volume method. This would be the correct answer if the patient had been seated in the sealed transparent box instead." },
      { "text": "Helium does not cross the alveolar-capillary membrane", "explanation": "Correct. Because helium is not absorbed across the alveolar-capillary membrane, all of it stays in the gas phase and is simply distributed through a larger volume once the lungs are added to the circuit. That allows the simple conservation relationship C1 × V1 = C2 × (V1 + V2) to be rearranged to V2 = (C1 × V1 ÷ C2) − V1, where V2 is the functional residual capacity. Subtracting the expiratory reserve volume from FRC then yields the residual volume." }
    ],
