      { "text": "FEV1/FVC of 90% with decreased residual volume and FRC", "explanation": "Correct. Her FEV1 of 2.8 L over an FVC of 3.1 L gives a ratio of about 90%, which is normal or above normal — the classic restrictive result, because stiff lungs with high elastic recoil empty rapidly even though they hold less. Reduced compliance limits expansion, so total lung capacity, functional residual capacity, and residual volume all fall. The dry cough, fine crackles, clubbing, and reduced chest expansion all fit reduced compliance rather than airway obstruction." },
      { "text": "FEV1/FVC of 90% with increased residual volume and increased FRC", "explanation": "Incorrect. The ratio is right but the volumes are inverted: residual volume and functional residual capacity rise in obstruction and fall in restriction. A stiff lung cannot be held open at end-expiration. This would be the correct answer if the ratio were preserved by some mechanism that also trapped gas, which stiff lungs do not do." }
