    "category": "Respiratory Physiology: Dead Space",
    "questionText": "In an experimental model, blood flow to one lung segment is abruptly interrupted while its airway remains fully patent. The animal's tidal volume, respiratory rate, and total minute ventilation are held constant by the ventilator, and the affected segment continues to fill and empty with every breath exactly as before. The conducting airways are structurally unchanged throughout. Which change in dead space occurs, and which measurement technique would reveal it?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Anatomic dead space rises; Fowler's nitrogen method would detect it", "explanation": "Incorrect. Anatomic dead space is fixed by the structure of the conducting airways, and the vignette specifies that those airways are unchanged. Fowler's method locates the boundary between airway gas and alveolar gas and would return the same value as before. This would be the correct answer if the intervention had enlarged or bypassed the conducting airways themselves." },
      { "text": "Anatomic dead space falls; Fowler's method would show a smaller area", "explanation": "Incorrect. Interrupting perfusion does nothing to the volume of the conducting airways, so the nitrogen-free area on a Fowler tracing would be unaltered. The change is entirely downstream of the terminal bronchioles. This would be the correct answer if the conducting airways had been partially occluded, reducing their volume." },
      { "text": "Physiologic dead space is unchanged; neither method would show a change", "explanation": "Incorrect. Alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused are alveolar dead space by definition, and alveolar dead space is one of the two components of physiologic dead space. Losing perfusion must therefore increase the total. This would be the correct answer if the segment had lost both its ventilation and its perfusion together." },
      { "text": "Physiologic dead space rises; the Bohr-Enghoff equation would detect it", "explanation": "Correct. The affected alveoli are now ventilated but not perfused, which is the definition of alveolar dead space, and physiologic dead space is anatomic plus alveolar. Because those units deliver no carbon dioxide, they dilute the carbon dioxide in expired gas, so the Bohr-Enghoff calculation VD/VT = (PACO2 − PECO2) ÷ PACO2 registers the rise. Fowler's nitrogen method would miss it entirely, because it measures only the conducting airways." },
      { "text": "Physiologic dead space falls; the Bohr-Enghoff ratio VD/VT would decrease", "explanation": "Incorrect. The direction is reversed: adding non-perfused but ventilated alveoli increases wasted ventilation and therefore raises VD/VT. A falling ratio would imply that more of each breath was participating in exchange. This would be the correct answer if perfusion had been restored to previously non-perfused units rather than removed." }
    ],
