    "id": 25,
    "category": "Respiratory Physiology: Measurement of Residual Volume",
    "questionText": "Two adults of similar height and weight undergo nitrogen washout on the same afternoon, each breathing 100% oxygen through a two-way valve while expired nitrogen is monitored. The first subject reduces her end-tidal nitrogen concentration below 2.5% within five minutes. The second subject requires twelve minutes to reach the same point, although his measured functional residual capacity turns out to be similar to hers. Which factor best explains the difference?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Subject 2 has a smaller FRC, so more breaths are needed to clear it", "explanation": "Incorrect. The vignette states that their functional residual capacities are similar, and in any case a smaller reservoir of nitrogen would be cleared faster rather than slower. Volume is not what differs between them. This would be the correct answer if the second subject had a markedly larger functional residual capacity." },
      { "text": "Subject 2 has higher airway resistance, slowing nitrogen clearance", "explanation": "Correct. Most people with a normal distribution of airway resistances reduce their expired end-tidal nitrogen concentration below 2.5% within about seven minutes, while those with higher resistance in their airways take longer to remove all the nitrogen. Poorly ventilated regions behind narrowed airways empty slowly and continue to release nitrogen long after well-ventilated regions have cleared. This is where obstructive pathology declares itself during what is nominally a volume measurement." },
      { "text": "Subject 2 has increased lung compliance, raising the nitrogen store", "explanation": "Incorrect. Compliance influences how readily the lung fills, but the vignette already establishes that their functional residual capacities are comparable, so the nitrogen store at the start of the test is similar. Clearance rate, not reservoir size, is what differs. This would be the correct answer if the second subject's lung volume were substantially larger." },
      { "text": "Subject 2 absorbed more nitrogen across the alveolar-capillary membrane", "explanation": "Incorrect. Absorption of nitrogen into blood would remove it from the lungs and shorten the washout, not prolong it, and this route is negligible during the test. Tracer gases are chosen for their inertness. This would be the correct answer if nitrogen were being cleared primarily through the circulation." },
      { "text": "Subject 2 was given a lower inspired oxygen concentration during testing", "explanation": "Incorrect. The protocol delivers 100% oxygen on inspiration through the two-way valve, and a deviation from that would be a technical error rather than a physiologic explanation. Nothing in the vignette suggests the circuit was set up differently. This would be the correct answer if the two subjects had been tested with different inspired gas mixtures." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: normal subjects wash end-tidal nitrogen below 2.5% within ~7 minutes; a prolonged washout signals increased airway resistance.",
