    "id": 27,
    "category": "Respiratory Physiology: Dead Space Measurement",
    "questionText": "A subject takes a single deep breath of 100% oxygen and then exhales slowly while a rapid nitrogen analyser samples the gas at the mouth. The tracing shows zero nitrogen at the very beginning of expiration, then a progressive rise, and finally a stable plateau that persists until the end of the breath. The technician marks the plateau and uses the areas on either side of the transition to calculate a volume. Which statement best explains the plateau?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "All dead-space gas has been washed out, leaving only alveolar gas", "explanation": "Correct. The oxygen breath completely fills the conducting airways while only partially displacing alveolar gas, so the first gas out contains no nitrogen at all. As alveolar gas begins to reach the analyser the nitrogen concentration rises, and once the dead space has been entirely cleared from the passages only alveolar gas remains, so the recorded concentration levels off at the alveolar value. The nitrogen-free area, expressed as a fraction of the whole expired breath, gives the anatomic dead space volume." },
      { "text": "Nitrogen has begun to diffuse from capillary blood back into alveoli", "explanation": "Incorrect. Nitrogen is inert and does not move across the alveolar-capillary membrane in quantities that could shape this tracing over a single breath. The rise and plateau reflect the sequence in which gas leaves the lung, not any diffusive exchange. This would be the correct answer if the plateau were generated by continuous delivery of tracer from the circulation." },
      { "text": "The subject has reached residual volume and airflow has ceased", "explanation": "Incorrect. The plateau appears well before the end of the maneuver and persists while gas is still flowing; if airflow had stopped, no further sampling would occur. Reaching residual volume also requires a maximal forced effort, which is not what is described. This would be the correct answer if the tracing ended abruptly rather than levelling off." },
      { "text": "Oxygen has been fully absorbed, so only the inspired nitrogen remains", "explanation": "Incorrect. The inspired gas was 100% oxygen and therefore contained no nitrogen to begin with, and oxygen uptake over a single breath cannot concentrate a gas that was never inhaled. The nitrogen detected comes from alveolar gas that the oxygen breath failed to displace. This would be the correct answer if the subject had inspired room air rather than pure oxygen." },
      { "text": "The conducting airways have refilled with atmospheric air containing nitrogen", "explanation": "Incorrect. During expiration gas flows outward, so the conducting airways cannot be refilling from the atmosphere; they will only do so on the next inspiration, after the measurement is complete. The plateau is a feature of gas leaving the lung, not entering it. This would be the correct answer if the analyser were sampling during the subsequent inspiration." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: dead-space gas lies nearest the mouth and is expired first, so early expired gas after a pure oxygen breath is nitrogen-free — the basis of Fowler's method.",
